<p class="diagram-cap">Nearby entry levels agree, triple costs barely matter, and a one-day delay still leaves a Sharpe of \(0.92\) on spot.</p>

<div class="insight">
  <div class="insight-lbl">The verdict</div>
  The edge <strong>survives without the roll</strong>. On spot prices, Brent vs WTI earned \(11\%\) a year on unseen data with a Sharpe of \(1.57\) (\(t = 2.99\)), and its trades are spread evenly across the month. The roll changed <em>when</em> the futures strategy traded, not <em>whether</em> there was an edge. Spot prices had a trap of their own: Brent is recorded in London and WTI in New York, which can create a gap that closes the next day by itself. Trading one day late removes that effect, and the Sharpe stayed at \(0.92\).
</div>
{% endpsec %}

{% psec 6, "What I'd Check Before Trading It", "What could still be wrong?", "Limits and next steps" %}
<p class="topic-intro">The result passed every test I set. These are the ones I'd still want to run before risking real money.</p>

<div class="sh">1. Trade the real thing</div>
<p class="np">You can't trade spot prices directly. A desk would trade Brent and WTI futures for the <strong>same delivery month</strong>, and swap both legs on the same day. That needs prices for each individual contract, which free data doesn't provide.</p>

<div class="sh">2. A few trades carry a lot</div>
<p class="np">The equity curve rises in steps. A handful of trades, such as those during the oil crash in early 2020, add a large share of the profit. I'd re-run it without the biggest five trades to see how much is left.</p>

<div class="sh">3. I tested a lot of ideas</div>
<p class="np">Four markets, three pairs and four stages. The more ideas you test, the more likely one looks good by luck, and Brent vs WTI was the best of three pairs. Rolling the training window forward year by year (walk-forward testing) would give more unseen years to judge it on.</p>

<div class="sh">4. Part of the edge may be timing</div>
<p class="np">Acting a day late cut the Sharpe from \(1.57\) to \(0.92\). Some of that loss may come from the London and New York recording times rather than real trading opportunities. Prices for both markets taken at the same moment would settle it.</p>

<div class="sh">The core code</div>
<div class="code">def positions_from_z(z, entry=2.0, exit_=0.5):
    """+1 = long the gap (buy A, sell B), -1 = short the gap, 0 = flat."""
    pos, state = np.zeros(len(z)), 0
    for i, v in enumerate(z):
        if np.isnan(v):
            state = 0
        else:
            if (state == 1 and v > -exit_) or (state == -1 and v < exit_):
                state = 0                      # gap is back to normal: close
            if state == 0:
                state = -1 if v > entry else (1 if v < -entry else 0)
        pos[i] = state
    return pos</div>

<div class="qbox">
  <div class="qbox-lbl">Skills used</div>
  <p class="qbox-body">The most useful thing I built wasn't a strategy. It was the habit of attacking my own best result until it either broke or held.</p>
